            <div xml:id="d62">

                <pb facs="0060" n="48" sameAs="frus:frus1941v01/pg48"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/12817: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Finland</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Schoenfeld</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Helsinki</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-07-04T18:00:00+02:00">July 4, 1941—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 10:25 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>267. My telegram No. 266 today. Following is text of my formulation, made at
                    President Ryti’s request, of his remarks to me today: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“In answer to my inquiry as to the policy of the Finnish Government in
                            the present situation of hostilities with the Soviet Union, His
                            Excellency President Ryti stated, and authorized me so to inform the
                            President of the United States, that Finland was a co-belligerent with
                            Germany in those hostilities and was seeking to achieve purely Finnish
                            aims. The strategic position of Finland after the conclusion in 1940 of
                            the previous hostilities with the Soviet Union was dangerously
                            vulnerable and it would be the purpose of Finland to correct that
                            condition for her own protection only. Aside from necessary military
                            understandings, in connection with the parallel operations now
                            proceeding in common with the German forces, no political agreements
                            exist between the Finnish and the German Governments. In view of the
                            restrictions upon imports of food as a result of the action of the
                            British Government, Finland welcomed the expected shipment of 25,000
                            tons of grain and other material aid including lubricants from Germany.
                            It is the view of President Ryti that the defeat of the Soviet Army and
                            the complete crushing of Bolshevism as a consequence thereof are in the
                            interest of the whole world, and particularly of Finland. But the
                            Finnish people have not departed and have no intention of departing from
                            their established national system of political and social
                            organization.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Schoenfeld</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
